AB  - In recent days, traffic accidents involving twowheeled motorcycle riders are attributed improper helmet usage, driving under influence of alcohol, or rider fatigue from extended travel times on the road. According to some studies, nearly $87 \%$ of two-wheeler deaths in accidents, either cyclists (bikes with engines) or motorcyclists (electric bikes) can be attributed to a rider failing to wear a helmet. This research presents an image processing-based Smart Helmet system that combines image processing for helmet detection, an IoT-based health and safety protection system for long-distance riding, and a real-time emergency communication system. The smart helmet image processing system uses a YOLOv8 based model detects if rider is wearing a helmet and keeps the bike’s gear system locked if not wearing a helmet (the bike can still be started, however). It is assumed to be helmet based on a comprehensive helmet framework being used (helmet), and incorporates alcohol detection, body temperature, and air quality sensors for the safety of long-distance riders. The helmet can contact emergency contacts rapidly using the GSM (GPRS) and GPS module, and sends location and details of the incident in its alert to the emergency contacts. The proposed model in this research is better than existing systems in regard to overall detection accuracy ($95.2 \%$ precision accuracy and $93.5 \%$ recall accuracy), and we can be confident that it will work well in practical situations based on our study. We believe we have addressed many shortcomings of previous research, which did not address multiple sensor detection systems and real-time measurable detection system; therefore, our research is comprehensive in providing improved rider safety. The proposed Smart Helmet can provide a means to enter the rider safety industry (for example, accidents), and reduce accidents (and people getting hurt), encourage safe driving habits, and pave the way for further applications, for instance with the mobile smart app with improved low-power sensors.
ER  - 

