AB  - Wearable technology allows real-time detection of the wearer and his/her surroundings, offering room for innovation in various fields. This paper presented wearable technology and industrial manufacturing to develop a smart safety helmet that facilitates enhanced workplace safety in industrial production. The helmet mitigates two significant hazards by integrating advanced sensor technologies and realtime communication systems: falls and toxic gas exposure. This system was founded on the ESP32 microcontroller, which uses sensors such as the MQ-2 for gas detection and the ADXL345 accelerometer to detect falls. The helmet's fall detection utilizes an accelerometer to monitor three-axis acceleration, while the gas-detecting system monitors levels of toxic gases such as LPG and smoke. During a crisis, the system shows immediate information and warnings, including GPS positioning, to the emergency contacts and the wearer to facilitate quick response and reduce injury severity. The industrial safety idea illustrates the potential of wearable technology in preventing hazards. The proposed system holds tremendous scalability and flexibility potential in various occupational safety environments and, as such, is an essential instrument for ensuring worker safety and resilience at work.
ER  - 

